From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Prism <prism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 17:58:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [Win] GStreamer media backend, for Media Source
 Extensions

The Windows port plays media through Media Foundation, which only handles
plain files: there is no Media Source Extensions backend, so sites that
stream with MSE (YouTube and most video sites) can't play video.

Add a USE_GSTREAMER option to the Windows port that builds WebKit's
GStreamer media player and its MSE support against GStreamer's MSVC
binaries:

- cmake/GStreamerWin.cmake finds GStreamer and GLib in GSTREAMER_ROOT
  without pkg-config, so the copies of zlib, libxml2 etc. that come with
  GStreamer aren't picked over vcpkg's.
- WTF builds its GLib smart pointers and helpers when USE(GSTREAMER) is on
  (GLib's types only; the event loop stays RunLoopWin).
- GLib's default main context, which bus signal watches use, is dispatched
  from a main RunLoop timer.
- fnmatch() comes from g_pattern_match_simple() on Windows.
- GStreamer replaces the Media Foundation player, which has no MSE support
  (DirectWrite stays linked for fonts).
- GRefPtr.h and GSpanExtras.h include GLib's headers on Windows instead of
  forward-declaring its functions, whose real declarations are dllimport.
- MSVC/clang-cl fixes in shared code: no WEBCORE_EXPORT on a member of an
  exported class (MediaSourcePrivate), intptr_t instead of ssize_t
  (MediaSource), atomic_flag default-initialized (AppendPipeline).
- GLib-only bits stay GLib-only: application name, RunLoop timer
  priorities, coordinated-graphics and media-stream includes.
- MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer's IsDuplicateSample is declared on ports
  without coordinated graphics too (triggerRepaint uses it).
- WebKit and its processes get GStreamer's include folders and libraries
  (WebCore's SharedBuffer.h includes GStreamer headers).
- Without coordinated graphics, the GStreamer player doesn't claim
  accelerated rendering: frames are painted through paint(), and
  triggerRepaint() asks for a repaint for each one. paint() doesn't skip
  frames on the page-visibility flag there, and logs why it doesn't
  paint; failed frame conversions are logged too.
- The page's tiles are painted through the GPU process, whose graphics
  context only takes video frames on Cocoa. Without coordinated graphics,
  paint() converts each frame to an image once and paints that image, and
  RemoteGraphicsContextProxy::drawVideoFrame() falls back to painting the
  frame as an image off Cocoa.
